WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.262
SQL does not operate only with TRUE and FALSE.

00:00:03.262 --> 00:00:08.717
When NULL is involved, SQL can produce a third state called UNKNOWN.

00:00:08.717 --> 00:00:13.647
NULL is not a value such as zero, false, or an empty string.

00:00:13.647 --> 00:00:19.627
It represents missing, unknown, or sometimes not-applicable information.

00:00:19.627 --> 00:00:26.342
Because SQL does not know the underlying value, many ordinary comparisons cannot be evaluated as definitely

00:00:26.342 --> 00:00:29.352
true or definitely false. Suppose a patient's age is NULL.

00:00:29.352 --> 00:00:37.372
If we ask whether age is greater than sixty-five, SQL cannot say yes, because the value is missing.

00:00:37.372 --> 00:00:43.602
But it also cannot say no, because the real age could be seventy.

00:00:43.602 --> 00:00:45.552
The result is UNKNOWN.

00:00:45.552 --> 00:00:51.662
The most important practical rule is that WHERE keeps only rows where the condition is TRUE.

00:00:51.662 --> 00:00:55.682
FALSE is removed, and UNKNOWN is also removed.

00:00:55.682 --> 00:00:59.442
This can make rows disappear in ways that surprise people.

00:00:59.442 --> 00:01:07.572
A customer with a missing subscription status may vanish from both “status equals Active” and “status not

00:01:07.572 --> 00:01:10.697
equal Active” queries, because neither comparison is definitely true.

00:01:10.697 --> 00:01:16.987
In a real data-quality workflow, UNKNOWN is often meaningful.

00:01:16.987 --> 00:01:22.662
Missing consent might mean “needs review.” Missing payment status might mean “investigate.” Missing diagnosis

00:01:22.662 --> 00:01:30.162
confirmation might mean “pending.” So do not think of UNKNOWN as an error.

00:01:30.162 --> 00:01:34.882
Think of it as SQL honestly saying: based on the information available,

00:01:34.882 --> 00:01:37.288
I cannot prove this condition either way.
